WEBVTT

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:24.540
Good morning, I am Shudeshna Sharkha. Today, we start the first lecture on machine learning.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:33.180
This is module 1, part a. Today, we will introduce machine learning, go through the basics of

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:40.100
the course, discuss the brief history of machine learning and discuss what learning

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:43.880
is about and some simple applications of machine learning.

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:53.320
So, first this is the overview of the course. The course is over 8 weeks and we will have

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:59.420
8 modules. The first module is introduction. In the second module, we will discuss about

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:05.240
linear regression and decision trees. Third module, instance-based learning and feature

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:11.340
selection. Fourth module, probability and base learning. Fifth module, support vector

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:16.900
machines. Sixth module, neural networks. Seventh module, we will do an introduction to

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:22.100
computational learning theory and possibly a little bit of ensemble learning. And then

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:27.160
the last module, we will talk about clustering. In the first module, that is introduction,

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:33.540
we will have four parts. Today, we will give a brief introduction. In the next lecture,

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:39.520
we will have discuss about different types of learning, supervised, unsupervised, etcetera.

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:45.700
Then, we will have the third module, where we will talk about hypothesis space and inductive

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:52.160
bias. Following this, we will talk about evaluation, training and test set and cross

00:01:52.160 --> 00:02:00.400
validation. First, I will like to start with a brief history of machine learning. A machine

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:08.310
that is intellectually capable as much as humans has always fired the imagination of

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:16.490
writers and also the early computer scientists, who were excited about artificial intelligence

00:02:16.490 --> 00:02:24.290
and machine learning. But the first machine learning system was developed in the 1950s.

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:32.550
In 1952, Arthur Samuel, who was at IBM, he developed a program for playing checkers.

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:41.970
The program was able to observe positions at the game and learn a model that gives better

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:49.770
moves for the machine player. The system played many games with the program and observed that

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:56.830
the program was able to play better in the course of time with getting more experience

00:02:56.830 --> 00:03:04.450
of board games. Samuel coined the term machine learning and he defined learning as a field

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:13.030
of study that gives computers the ability without being explicitly programmed. In 1957,

00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:22.490
Rosenblatt proposed the perceptron. Perceptron is the simple neural network unit. It was

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:30.650
a very exciting discovery at that time. Rosenblatt made the following statement. The perceptron

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:37.370
is designed to illustrate some of the fundamental properties of intelligent systems in general

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:44.310
without becoming too deeply enmeshed in the special and frequently unknown conditions

00:03:44.570 --> 00:03:53.550
which hold for particular biological organisms. But after three years, Widrow and Hoff came up

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:59.730
with the delta learning rule that is used for learning perceptrons. It was used as

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:05.430
a procedure for training perceptron. It is also known as the least square problem.

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:16.570
The combination of these ideas created a good linear classifier. However, the work along this line

00:04:16.570 --> 00:04:26.490
suffered a setback when Minsky in 1969 came up with the limitations of perceptron. He

00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:34.010
showed that the XOR problem could not be represented by perceptron and such inseparable data

00:04:34.010 --> 00:04:42.190
distributions cannot be handled. And following this Minsky's work, Burel Network Research

00:04:42.190 --> 00:04:52.850
went dormant up until the 1980s. In the meantime, in the 1970s, machine learning

00:04:52.850 --> 00:05:00.030
followed the symbolic type of artificial intelligence, good old fashioned artificial intelligence.

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:08.890
Those types of learning algorithms were developed. Concept induction was worked on and then J. R.

00:05:09.110 --> 00:05:17.610
Quinlan in 1986 came up with decision tree learning specifically the ID-3 algorithm. It

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:25.030
was also released as a software and it had simplistic rules contrary to the black box

00:05:25.030 --> 00:05:32.390
of neural networks and it became quite popular. After ID-3, many alternatives or improvement

00:05:32.390 --> 00:05:40.270
to ID-3 were developed such as cart, regression trees and it is still one of the very popular

00:05:40.270 --> 00:05:46.910
topics in machine learning. During this time, symbolic natural language processing also

00:05:46.910 --> 00:05:54.070
became very popular. In 1980s, advanced decision trees and rule learning were developed.

00:05:54.390 --> 00:06:00.730
Learning, planning, problem solving was there. At the same time, there was a resurgence of

00:06:00.730 --> 00:06:08.730
neural network. The intuition of multi-layer perceptron was suggested by Wehrbos in 1981

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:16.110
and neural network specific back propagation algorithm was developed. Back propagation

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:22.430
is the key ingredient of today's neural network architectures. With those ideas,

00:06:22.430 --> 00:06:29.990
neural network research became popular again and there was an acceleration in 1985-86 when

00:06:29.990 --> 00:06:36.530
neural network researchers presented the idea of MLP that is multi-layer perceptron with

00:06:36.530 --> 00:06:42.970
practical BP training, Rumelhardt, Hinton, Williams, H. Nielsen, where some of the

00:06:42.970 --> 00:06:50.270
scientists who worked in this area. During this time, theoretical framework of machine

00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:56.330
learning was also presented, the Allianz-Pak learning theory which stands for probably approximately

00:06:56.330 --> 00:07:02.870
correct learning it was developed and the focus shifted on experimental methodologies.

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:11.070
In the 90s, machine learning embraced statistics to a large extent. It was during this time

00:07:11.070 --> 00:07:17.090
that support vector machines were proposed. It was a machine learning breakthrough and

00:07:17.090 --> 00:07:25.070
the support vector machines were proposed by Wapnick and Cortes in 1995 and SVM had very

00:07:25.070 --> 00:07:28.210
strong theoretical standing and empirical results.

00:07:29.810 --> 00:07:36.070
Then another strong machine learning model was proposed by Freund and Schappeyer in 1997

00:07:36.070 --> 00:07:43.870
which was part of what we called ensembles or boosting and they came up with a algorithm

00:07:43.870 --> 00:07:50.830
called Adaboost by which they could create a strong classifier from an ensemble of weak

00:07:50.830 --> 00:07:58.730
classifiers. The kernelized version of SVM was proposed near 2000s which was able to

00:07:58.730 --> 00:08:05.550
exploit the knowledge of convex optimization, generalization and kernels. Another ensemble

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:13.790
model was explored by Breiman in 2001 that ensembles multiple decision trees where each

00:08:13.790 --> 00:08:20.950
of them is curated by a random subset of instances. This is called random forest. During this

00:08:20.950 --> 00:08:30.180
time base net learning was also proposed. Then a neural network took another damage

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:35.960
by the work of Hawkriter. Hawkriter showed that gradient loss after the saturation

00:08:35.960 --> 00:08:42.920
of neural network unit happens when we apply back propagation so that after a certain

00:08:42.920 --> 00:08:52.740
number of epochs neural networks are inclined to overfit. But as we come closer today we see

00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:59.740
that neural networks are again very much popular. We have a new era in neural network called

00:08:59.740 --> 00:09:11.620
deep learning and this phrase refers to neural network with many deep layers. This

00:09:11.620 --> 00:09:19.700
is the conjunction of many different discoveries by people like Hinton, Lecun, Benjio, Andrew

00:09:19.700 --> 00:09:27.460
NG and other researchers. At the same time if you look at certain applications where machine

00:09:27.460 --> 00:09:35.280
learning has come to the public forefront in 1994 the first self-driving car made a road

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:45.800
test. In 1997 Deep Blue beat the world champion Garry Kasparov in the game of chess. In 2009

00:09:45.800 --> 00:09:54.720
we have Google building self-driving cars. In 2011 Watson again from IBM won the popular

00:09:54.720 --> 00:10:03.480
game of Geoparty. 2014 we see human visions are passed by ML systems. In 2014-15 we

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:12.480
find that machine translation systems driven by neural networks are very good and they

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:19.440
are better than the other statistical machine translation systems. There are certain concepts

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:28.300
and certain technology which are making headlines. Now in machine learning we have GPUs which

00:10:28.300 --> 00:10:36.380
are enabling the use of machine learning and deep neural networks. There is the cloud, there

00:10:36.380 --> 00:10:41.820
is availability of big data and the field of machine learning is very exciting now.

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:51.720
So, with this brief introduction of machine learning history we will now discuss what is

00:10:52.680 --> 00:10:59.980
machine learning? What is a learning algorithm? First let us look at how a machine learning

00:10:59.980 --> 00:11:09.840
solution differs from a programmatic solution. When you have a program or an algorithm to

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:20.940
solve a problem this is how you use the computer. This is your computer and you

00:11:20.940 --> 00:11:30.320
write a program. The program takes data as the input and the program produces output.

00:11:34.310 --> 00:11:45.370
On the other hand when you are using machine learning and you have the computer you are

00:11:45.370 --> 00:11:55.320
feeding the data input as well as examples of output. So, you are putting examples of

00:11:55.320 --> 00:12:04.520
input output data and you are getting a program or a model with which you can solve subsequent

00:12:05.160 --> 00:12:11.740
tasks. So, this is what learning is about. So, with this let us come to the formal

00:12:11.740 --> 00:12:32.670
definition of machine learning. Learning is the ability to improve one's behavior

00:12:32.920 --> 00:13:05.340
with experience. So, it is about building computer systems that automatically improve with experience

00:13:06.050 --> 00:13:13.500
and we have to discuss what are the fundamental laws that govern the learning processes.

00:13:14.780 --> 00:13:31.990
Machine learning explores algorithms that learn from data, build models from data

00:13:39.460 --> 00:13:49.240
and this model can be used for different tasks. For example, model can be used for prediction,

00:13:53.630 --> 00:14:17.640
decision making or solving tasks. We will discuss a formal definition of machine learning

00:14:17.640 --> 00:14:31.280
as given by Tom Mitchell and this is a definition that is followed very popularly. So, Mitchell's

00:14:41.450 --> 00:15:08.590
it says that a computer program is said to learn, what does it learn from? It learns from

00:15:08.590 --> 00:15:20.270
experience. So, the computer program learns from experience E. So, E is the experience

00:15:20.270 --> 00:15:34.720
of the computer program users. It is past data with respect to some class of tasks

00:15:35.020 --> 00:16:16.420
T, performance measured P. It is performance on tasks in T, on tasks in T as measured by

00:16:16.420 --> 00:16:42.620
P improves with experience E. So, learning is about using experience data that is from

00:16:42.620 --> 00:16:49.920
past problem solving data and there is a task, the task belongs to a class of tasks

00:16:49.940 --> 00:16:58.500
T and the tasks are evaluated by performance measured P and the machine is said to learn

00:16:58.970 --> 00:17:09.440
tasks in T if its performance at tasks as measured by P improves with experience E. So,

00:17:09.480 --> 00:17:23.150
what we see is that the components of a learning algorithm are follows is the behavior

00:17:23.150 --> 00:17:30.830
of the task behavior or the task that the learning program is seeking to improve. For

00:17:30.830 --> 00:17:44.870
example, there are different types of tasks like prediction, classification, acting in

00:17:44.870 --> 00:17:58.530
an environment etcetera. The second component is the data or the experience. So, the

00:17:58.530 --> 00:18:07.190
experience is also called the data. This is what is used for improving the experience of

00:18:07.190 --> 00:18:15.510
the task and then there is a measure of improvement P. For example, you might want to increase

00:18:15.510 --> 00:18:23.490
accuracy in prediction or you might want to have new skills to the agent which it

00:18:23.490 --> 00:18:29.910
did not earlier process or improve efficiency of problem solving corresponding to this

00:18:29.910 --> 00:18:40.650
you can define the performance measure. So, based on this definition we can look at a learning

00:18:40.650 --> 00:18:54.750
system as a box. So, this is our learning system it is a box to which we feed the

00:18:54.750 --> 00:19:09.490
experience or the data and there is a problem or a problem or a problem or a problem

00:19:09.510 --> 00:19:21.830
a task that requires solution and you can also give background knowledge which will help

00:19:22.130 --> 00:19:33.760
the system and for this problem or this task the learning program comes up with an answer

00:19:34.520 --> 00:19:44.080
or a solution and it is corresponding performance can be measured. So, this is the schematic

00:19:44.080 --> 00:19:53.400
diagram of a machine learning system or a learner system. Inside there are two components two

00:19:53.400 --> 00:20:11.750
main components the learner L and the reasoner. See the learner takes the experience and from

00:20:11.750 --> 00:20:19.380
that it can also take the background knowledge and from this the learner builds models and

00:20:22.080 --> 00:20:30.500
this models can be used by the reasoner which given a task finds the solution to the task.

00:20:31.500 --> 00:20:37.940
So, the learner takes experience and background knowledge and learns a model and the reasoner

00:20:37.940 --> 00:20:45.520
works with the model and given a new problem or task it can come up with the solution

00:20:45.520 --> 00:20:53.380
to the task and the performance measure corresponding to this. Now, we will like to look at some examples

00:20:53.380 --> 00:20:59.720
of machine learning system. So, machine learning there are many domains and applications of

00:20:59.720 --> 00:21:04.840
machine learning. For example, in medicine you can use machine learning to diagnose

00:21:04.840 --> 00:21:12.420
a disease where the input are the symptoms, lab measurements, test results, DNA tests

00:21:12.420 --> 00:21:22.040
etcetera. Output could be one of a set of possible diseases or none of them. For doing this one

00:21:22.040 --> 00:21:30.060
can data mine historical medical records and learn which future patients will respond

00:21:30.060 --> 00:21:37.260
best to which treatments. Another domain is computer vision where given an image you

00:21:37.260 --> 00:21:45.060
want to find out what objects appear in an image and where the objects appear in an image.

00:21:45.680 --> 00:21:54.420
A third domain robot control one can use machine learning to design autonomous mobile robots

00:21:54.700 --> 00:22:04.820
that learn to navigate from their own experience. Then in the domain of natural language processing

00:22:05.180 --> 00:22:14.120
one can detect where entities are mentioned in natural language and detect what facts are

00:22:14.120 --> 00:22:21.220
expressed in natural language. One can look at a product or movie review and find out

00:22:21.220 --> 00:22:28.620
if it is positive, negative or neutral that is the sentiment on the review. Other applications

00:22:28.620 --> 00:22:36.720
in NLP include speech recognition, machine translation etcetera. In the financial domain one can try

00:22:36.720 --> 00:22:45.420
to predict if a stock will rise or fall. One can predict if a user will click on an advertisement

00:22:45.420 --> 00:22:54.080
or not. There are many applications in business intelligence. You want to robustly forecast

00:22:54.570 --> 00:23:02.440
product sales quantities, taking seasonality and trend into account, identify cross selling

00:23:02.440 --> 00:23:11.280
promotional opportunities, identify price sensitivity of a consumer product, optimize product location

00:23:11.280 --> 00:23:18.650
at a supermarket shelf and so on. Then there are other applications such as fraud detection,

00:23:18.650 --> 00:23:26.710
credit card fraud detection, understand consumer sentiment, forecast women's conviction rates

00:23:26.710 --> 00:23:34.930
based on external macroeconomic factors etcetera. So, these are some of the many many applications

00:23:34.930 --> 00:23:40.710
of machine learning. Machine learning is a part of many products and systems that

00:23:40.710 --> 00:23:48.630
we routinely use. If you look at the box that we drew for machine learning systems

00:23:48.650 --> 00:23:58.450
and discuss how we can go about creating a learner these are the following steps. First

00:23:58.450 --> 00:24:13.990
of all we choose the training experience or the training data. Then we choose the target

00:24:13.990 --> 00:24:32.780
function or how we want to represent the model. So, this is what we want to learn the target

00:24:32.780 --> 00:24:41.600
function that is to be learned. For example, if you are trying to write a machine learning

00:24:41.600 --> 00:24:48.220
system to play the game of checkers the target function would be given a board position what

00:24:48.220 --> 00:24:55.810
move to take and then we want to have the class of function that we will use. The task

00:24:55.810 --> 00:25:02.190
is given a board position what move to take and we will design the function as a function

00:25:02.190 --> 00:25:08.070
of the input and we have to decide what type of function we will use whether we will use

00:25:08.070 --> 00:25:16.610
a linear function or some other representation. So, we choose how to represent the target function

00:25:33.600 --> 00:25:58.360
and finally, we choose a learning algorithm to infer the target function. So, the learning

00:25:58.360 --> 00:26:09.260
algorithm will explore the possible function parameters so that based on the training experience

00:26:09.260 --> 00:26:16.820
it can come up with the best function given its computational limitations. So, what is

00:26:16.820 --> 00:26:25.240
very important in the designing of a learning algorithm is how to represent the target function

00:26:25.240 --> 00:26:33.500
before that what is important is how to represent the training experience. So, the training experience

00:26:33.500 --> 00:26:43.460
as we will see can be expressed in the terms of features of the domain and then we have

00:26:43.460 --> 00:26:50.520
to decide how to represent the target function. So, we want to come up with the appropriate

00:26:50.520 --> 00:26:57.760
class of functions on the features. So, you have to decide the class of the functions and

00:26:58.440 --> 00:27:04.300
when we are trying to find this class of the functions we have to make very important decision.

00:27:04.840 --> 00:27:11.400
We can go for a very powerful function class which is very complex can represent complex

00:27:11.400 --> 00:27:19.600
concepts, but if we choose a powerful rich representation if we choose a rich representation

00:27:19.600 --> 00:27:33.090
of the class of functions then we can represent complex function, but and it will be more useful

00:27:33.090 --> 00:27:46.040
for subsequent problem solving, but it may be more difficult to learn. So, richer representations

00:27:46.840 --> 00:27:55.580
are able to represent many types of classes including complex classes solve complex problems,

00:27:55.580 --> 00:28:02.020
but are more difficult to learn and the components of representation as we have said are the

00:28:02.020 --> 00:28:08.800
features or the attributes of the domain or the vocabulary on which we define the functions.

00:28:09.360 --> 00:28:15.920
So, we have the features and then we have the class of functions which we also call

00:28:15.920 --> 00:28:27.000
the hypothesis language. We will talk more about this in the next lecture and when

00:28:27.000 --> 00:28:34.460
we in the course of this course as we study different learning algorithms you can keep these

00:28:34.460 --> 00:28:40.000
steps in mind. With this we come to the end of introduction. Thank you very much.

